member.summary:
Gets or sets how multiple subtitles of the same media file are synced.
normal = one at a time, audio analysed every run;
parallel = up to  subtitles at once;
fast = reuse the speech analysis of the first run for the remaining
subtitles of that file (identical results, ~3x less work per extra subtitle).

Reads text subtitle tracks straight out of a Matroska file, touching as little data as the container allows.
ffmpeg (and any generic demuxer) walks every cluster looking for a subtitle that takes a few kilobytes, so extraction costs a full-file read — minutes on a large remux. This reader uses the container's own structures instead:
the SeekHead to jump straight to Tracks and Cues, without walking past clusters;
the Cues index (read in one bulk pass) to find the clusters holding the track;
block headers only, seeking over every payload that is not this track's — the difference between reading 60 GB of video and reading a few megabytes;
a metadata-only cluster scan when the index has no entries for the track, rather than handing a 60 GB file back to ffmpeg.
Only text subtitle codecs are handled (S_TEXT/UTF8, S_TEXT/ASS, S_TEXT/SSA). Anything it cannot decode with certainty — laced or compressed blocks, unknown codecs, malformed EBML — returns false so the caller can fall back to ffmpeg.
